  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 102 AM EST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nA shortwave ridge axis slides across MI today. After a weak and \nmoisture-starved trof moves thru this evening, a broader upper ridge \nbuilds back in by late Tuesday. This is occurring in advance of a \nsharp shortwave trof exiting the Great Basin, and a trailing upper \nlow heading toward the OR coast. The Great Basin shortwave will \neventually knock down the ridge axis a bit, when it likely crosses \nthe northern lakes region late this week. It is also likely to \nbe starting to shear out when it gets here.\n\nBut until then, surface high pressure slowly moving from IL now to \nNC/VA Thursday, will be in total control. \n\nKeeping an eye on fog potential early today. Our nights are long in \nmid Nov, and this is the one night with light winds. Going forward, \nthe pressure gradient tightens as the surface high passes to our s \nand se. Our nights will tend to keep a bit of a sw-erly breeze. Well-\nsheltered locales may still have a chance of decoupling tonight and \nbeyond, but it will be a challenge. Otherwise, lots of relatively \nwarm and dry air moving northward. Current forecast max temps are \nnot threatening record highs, until maybe just maybe Saturday. \nPlenty of near 50 thru the 50s for highs this week across northern \nMI.\n\nOur next chance for rain, and it isn't much, is on Thursday. Decent \nbit of forcing potentially provided by the ejecting shortwave, but \nmoisture fields remain poor. A small chance of showers for part of \nChippewa Co is about all the current grids can muster Thursday. A \nbetter chance for rain develops this coming weekend, as a broad area \nof low pressure evolves in the central high plains and then lifts \nne. System is mature/occluded by the time gets to our neighborhood, \nbut windy/damp wx is a distinct possibility next weekend.",
